a cost onto  young people  and the result of that is that three  former chancellors are complaining in  public now  about the extent of  where this raid comes from first it's a  manifesto break as as philip hammond has  pointed out and uh that's a that's a  commitment that's been given by the  conservative party at the last election  there have been mutterings to say well  if a manifesto  pledge is going to be broken then maybe  there should be yet another election but  clearly the challenge would be how do  you balance the books and um by taxing  national insurance which effectively is  a tax on jobs  you you suffocate the need and the  ability to be creating jobs particularly  for younger workers particularly  unskilled workers but we should be  incentivizing so it seems that it's the  wrong the wrong way around  and that that revolt that the prime  minister is said to be facing is going  to be a standoff perhaps to deflect some  of the attention away from afghanistan  but right now i think it's about how  economically literate and numerate is  the government and be able to understand  these really big macroeconomic problems  and i think the jury's out on that in  terms of the handling of the last couple  of years but it's the principle  challenge now to get the  the book started balance  meanwhile jasmine we read that three  former chancellors have been critical of  the the plan also  yeah so boris johnson is under quite a  lot of political pressure at the moment  over this issue so it was something that  he pledged to fix when he became pm but  obviously with the pandemic it's really  exacerbated the focus on social care  we've got a situation where we've got an  increasingly aging population but we've  been slashing care funding and so now  the sector's really at a crunch point  and so johnson's in quite a difficult  position because it's clear that  something needs to be done but no one's  no one's clear on what the solutions are  and he's getting a lot of pushback from  within his own party there are some who  are opposed to it on you know  ideological terms they don't believe the  conservatives should be increasing tax  on national insurance they don't like  the idea of breaking a manifesto pledge  they think it sends a really bad message  to the electorate that the party will  break its promises and then there are  others who you know raise concerns about  the fairness of the policy actually  should the cost fall on those who were  younger those who you know have fewer  assets such as houses or those who are  working age and so there's a real  tension between how you solve this you  know this huge crisis and i think  johnson is going to have to convince not  only back ventures but also the public  but if it's going to be the case that  there is a increase in national  insurance and so they're going to be  moved on you know from the individual to  the taxpayer then johnson really needs  to put forward an argument and put  forward his case for that and i do think  overall we need to have you know a  public debate about social care i think  in  cross contrast to the nhs for example  which is deeply ingrained in our social  consciousness we were saving the nhs  clapping for the nhs during the pandemic  i think most people don't really  understand social care and only really  come into contact with it if an elderly  relative needs to use it and so i think  we need a radical overhaul as to how we  think about the sector um peter we here  tonight that the prime minister will be  pushing ahead with his plans despite the  the opposition um how difficult a battle  do you think he's got on his hands come  monday  well jasmine is exactly right that it's  it's how to square the circle you know  the problem is is that i thought that  the polling numbers will be telling uh  boris johnson and the conservative party  that older people tend to vote  conservative and younger people are more  likely to be center-left and therefore  they're not really a constituency that  he's going to worry too much about they  turn out less often in elections but the  way in which the young have been hurt  and hit by cuts education  through with extra funding problems like  the national insurance and other taxes  you know it's a real battle for how this  country gets uh united together we're  split and have been split the last five  or six years around brexit around europe  and not europe and i think this now  between young and old becomes another  issue alongside the north and south  leveling up problems so you know there  are lots of these divisions and the real  question i think is is boris johnson a  prime minister who fundamentally can  unite or is it that kind of trumpian  role of antagonized politics where you  only bring along your own supporters and  you don't really care what the other  side do and that is the sort of the  zeitgeist a lot of democracies right now  not just here in the uk but you need i  think as a prime minister to conciliate  you need to be able to offer fig leaves  you need to listen to criticism and  occasionally back down and i think that  the problem about any prime minister  probably like boris johnson at the  moment if you feel that you're cornered  you know that may not necessarily the  best way to make um constructive  decisions jasmine let's move on to the  telegraph and this story which is going  to be i'm sure i predict a really really  um  tough talking point to do with the 12 to  15 year olds and being vaccinated  children as young as 12 and we're told  will be able to overrule their parents  if they object to them receiving the  vaccine  yeah so this is quite a sensitive topic  because obviously the jcbi last week  they said that they weren't going to  approve vaccinations for the  vast majority of 12 to 15 year olds but  they've left it to the chief medical  officers and so it's still sort of  influx but there are these sort of  practical questions about what happens  if 